{"author":{"id":"ad87ffa09351c4e5a412ec8ee1546e86b314f33d6f28629bb4bcfe7b2290924d"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794620404,"nonce":"kmmqah/5BaOLVB1vh2Akv+4tam4=","title":"Lemma counts per text, for coverage bars and choosing texts","message":"Ingest writes each text's distinct lemmas with how often each occurs, { lemma_id: count }, as its own small part beside the analysis (e.g. doc/{id}/lemmas/{name}.json.br), named after the analysis version it came from.\n\nCounts tokens with a lemma_id, leaving out PUNCT, NUM, PROPN, SYM and X, so the reader and the API count the same way. Texts analysed before this get the part worked out from their analysis when it's first asked for.\n\nWhat it's for:\n- Bars on the reading list: the user's Durable Object copies a text's counts into a document_lemma table when it goes on the list (one INSERT ... FROM json_each), and coverage is one GROUP BY joining it with word (and card, if active words count by FSRS recall probability). Store which analysis version each copy came from, so a re-analysed text's copy is refreshed.\n- Coverage = the share of counted tokens whose lemma is known; ignored lemmas are left out. About 95% coverage is readable with help and 98% comfortable (Hu \u0026 Nation 2000, Schmitt et al. 2011).\n- Later, choosing texts that suit a user: per-language lemma frequency ranks (from our own texts or e.g. wordfreq, CC BY-SA), and in D1 per text the rank needed for 95% and 98% coverage. D1 narrows by the user's frequency frontier, then the user DO re-ranks the top ~50 exactly from their counts.\n\nThe bar on the open text doesn't need any of this: the reader has the bundle's tokens and all the user's words.\n\nCost to check: DO SQLite bills rows read and written. Adding a text writes one row per lemma, and a list view reads about twice its lemmas. Whether json_each over one JSON row counts as rows read is unknown.","files":null}]}